  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nContinued drier conditions will last through the remainder of the \nweekend and through most of next week in South Texas outside of \nisolated seabreeze showers and thunderstorms. Some residual energy \nfrom Hurricane Simon over in the Pacific will look to ride around \nthe periphery of the ridge Monday with South Texas on the right \nflank of the area of low pressure. Thanks to onshore flow ahead of \nthis will return to the region allowing for deep moisture pooling \n(PWATs 2.0\"+ in the region. The presence of these to factors will \nlikely lead to a low chance for unsettled weather in the region \nMonday. Coverage will likely not be widespread as guidance is \nportraying activity being confined to the eastern Coastal Plains and \nVictoria Crossroads. \n\nAfter Monday, we will back to expecting daily seabreeze convection \nwith isolated coverage. Warm temperatures will remain in place until \nThursday. This is where the forecast is remaining tricky, some \nguidance brings a strong cold front down into the region (Canadian \nand the GFS). Whereas the ECMWF, portrays a slightly weaker solution \ninto South Texas where the front washes out and the brunt of the \nfront is located to the east towards HGX's CWA. GEFS temperature \nanomalies maintains the colder solution in South Texas with the \nlatest run. With the differences in the guidance remaining confidence \nunfortunately will remain low at this time, though we continue to \nmonitoring the trends over the next couple days. \n\nOtherwise, today we likely still see continued minor coastal \nflooding in the region through potentially Wednesday. P-ETTS \nmaintains water levels around 2.0 ft MSL through Wednesday and \npotentially lingering further into the latter portions of the\nweek.",
